  "reviewer_notes": {
    "probe": "A narrow central ribbon and finer companion runnel start at the upper basalt lip. The lower cataract divides into three brighter flows with a calm blue-grey pool ahead. The continuous ground rises into distant ridges.",
    "timeline": "The camera glides low toward the falls. Animated ripples run down the divided lower apron, thinner foam streams travel across the pool, and mist shifts around the impact. Weathered green patches run between the basalt layers.",
    "angles": "The wide view includes the basalt OBJ in the pool. The oblique view shows the connected upstream ridges, two rock ledges, side banks, and pool as one valley set. The terrain and water meshes have finite ends visible at these proof angles. The overhead view fits the pink camera track and both ledges.",
    "references": "The palette pairing keeps the blue-grey water, pale foam, dark basalt and muted greens of the wider canyon photo. The narrow upper ribbon, high rock drop and moss patches carry over from the subject photo. The camera angle, stronger mountain relief and darker greens differ from the photograph.",
    "clip": "Sampled the decoded clip twice per second. White streaks advect down the curtains, ripples travel away from the impact, and foreground parallax and the growing falls show the forward camera movement. The bank and shoreline remain continuous through the shot.",
    "jev_look_items": "The pool is muted glacial blue-green and can be checked at full size. Low white spray crosses the foot of the falls and rises behind its bright front edge. The draft retains visible noise in the moving water and the small spray highlights."
  },
